{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner completed the whole R3 revision2 reading at exactde9a1d64b1788cb61ef048e2f6b753a7c8649fd9 under handoff8c5caee1 and correctionf6c4d14b. I read all1087 main-note lines and the complete26-line correction matrix. Main71488 bytes,SHA-2567f6d72c374ec7f2740b9aa2ba47269cc9466c0efcc3a894d03d183a39089cc6a; matrix8318 bytes,SHA-256fcfa7643925e0843c6a42aae2d29675b9ea5bcd65ddb968b55bf10df71d747d5. The723-line84111-byte source-facts note is byte-identical to the fully read draft1, SHA-25612db56d1ba4a9681e47408ebfc7cbfa75495163592a3e54bed4b85f71e76f8e0. Whitespace checking passed. The bounded read-start grace windows, separate terminal result and owner progress, unknown-create retention, per-hold isolation, complete eligibility statement and conditional fence proposal address substantial earlier corrections. This is internal planning reading, not an independent verdict, adoption or source authorization.\n\nSeven remaining groups should be made concrete in the corrected exact design and matrix before R3 independent filing. Preserve the complete browser/durable-work outcomes and all-component retirement; do not add a multiplied fault matrix or a new Jam gate.\n\n1. Workspace issuance and private access (4.2,5.3). The opening sentence says all four operations need the controller/admin device key, while workspace-token issuance is by the hold holder, who can be an autonomous agent with its own member key. Separate a device's authority to retrieve raw private resources or authorize export from the trusted service's authority to provision/renew the agent's hold credential under a recorded start/hold and current agent/controller grant. Name each signer and receiver, the authority check and custody; a closed laptop cannot require fresh human signatures for ordinary continued hosted work. For mint recovery, return the same known record or retain an unknown mint; a new step requires its own authorized attempt and never settles the first. Do not silently mint again from an identical signed operation. State the end/recheck rule for an ongoing private stream/export, not only request admission, and domain/audience-bind direct requests to the exact task/repository incarnation and action. Preserve direct owner/admin private access, not a member handle or delegation.\n\n2. Hold, report and runtime consistency (5.1,5.2,7). Section5.2 says an ended holder can no longer report; section7 correctly allows a report after hold expiry if the immutable commit was already staged, because the commitment still owns responsibility. Distinguish a permitted report of that exact staged commit from a forbidden new push/stage; handover changes the performer and is different from hold expiry. Apply the same distinction to the 'no report can follow' race and keep grant/controller grace explicit. Identify forks, environments, snapshots, tokens and checkpoint guards using the exact lane incarnation, hold and runtime instance; the named fork path currently contains only scopeID and hold itemID while the table promises no reuse. The runtime must recheck the local hold's due time and exact current instance before dispatch, rather than treating membership's grace window as a lease extension. Explain same-holder restore of complete checkpoint/Git closure into a new hold's fork, and old instance callbacks/processes that remain unknown. No replacement retry clears their outside-effect debt.\n\n3. Staging's admission and ownership (6.2). It is a real write to the canonical repository before the act is admitted, not a read-only preparation. State what recorded authority/operation permits it before token mint and dispatch, and how a rejected, expired or revoked proposer remains unable to cause an unauthorized write. Agree the ledger/effect boundary explicitly with R1 instead of treating its permission as already granted. The create-only ref is keyed by lane scopeID and commit, so two attempts or acts for the same commit share it: specify matching-existing-ref recovery, exact content/incarnation, multiple owners and deletion/retry fencing. An older refused operation must not delete an accepted operation's root. Keep live accepted reports and pending integrations, not just manifests/reviews/jobs, pinned until consumers and replay retention no longer need the objects. Include separate source-fork and canonical token duties, provenance and complete immutable Git-object closure. Staging repeatability proves neither an unknown token settled nor an arbitrary deletion safe.\n\n4. Founding register (3.8,6.1,8.3). 'The register claim and directory genesis are made together' spans the deployment register and directory storage. Define their actual local owners and a recoverable claimed/pending/active order; do not assume an atomic transaction across Durable Objects. Bind the claim to the exact physical Git repository/namespace and directory incarnation, with durable identity preventing two intents or imports from producing writers for the same branch. State crash after claim/before genesis, lost result, storage loss/restoration and loss of the register itself; absence of its state cannot prove no earlier writer or credential exists. Activation follows the durable claim and confirmed membership, rules and destination needed for the action. Fresh founding uses a genuinely new host resource, not just a new nonce pointing at an old unresolved repository. Keep bootstrap policy/recovery-key bytes and the register proof replay-visible or explicitly trusted. This is founding-only coordination, not a new queue for ordinary lane acts.\n\n5. Publication message and proof details (6.3-6.10). Keep the improved single-result/progress design, but give an exact state mapping for every queued/reserved/unresolved/not-reserved/published/aborted update into P and G, including refuse-before-reserve and withdraw-before-arrival. Bind withdraw and progress to the original merge fact, full P/G ScopeRefs and destination-owned publication item, with revision derived from its sealed sender history; not just a mutable current proposal or a body number. A sender cannot withdraw another lane's operation. A terminal item cannot be reopened by a late higher-position update; exact duplicates return the original receipt. Every unknown grant/dispatch remains separately accounted. For the fence, name both the original's terminal outcome/receipt duty and the fence's outcome when the original wins; neither dangling fence attempts nor the opposite winning attempt disappear. Keep the ordinary 'hold' rule until adoption and preserve unrelated-lane progress.\n\n6. Check failure and real-host evidence (3.11,10,12). Define a durable run-error/attention outcome when runner/checkout fails and no check result is signed, so the browser does not wait on a model statement or silently drop a required check. Bind job origin to the exact change definition/recorded request, admitted configuration bytes and tree, authenticating what the service executes; an entry that happens to look like a job is insufficient. Late/refused results need durable recovery without rerunning the computation. State H1/H2/H4 as reported operational assumptions or supported contracts at the chosen host boundary; a finite successful race/readback witness establishes those cases, not universal guarantees. In particular detecting an unexpected head on the next reservation cannot retroactively make an erroneous final result safe. Keep stale-read cases under the stated ownership/order assumptions and retain uncertainty when the required fact is not established. Do not claim a probe has run or introduce an extra provider/Jam approval ceremony.\n\n7. Settlement and reset (8.2,8.3). Stop or fence new ordinary effects/writers before taking the settlement census; otherwise a clean snapshot can be invalidated before deletion/reset. Pending dispatches, gateway grants, runtime processes, outboxes and cleanup actors must be accounted for, and the owner/readers/storage needed for real late outcomes must survive until their duties settle. Inventory Artroom's identified resources across the configured namespaces without treating unrelated account resources as owned. Apply the operation/readback rules only with their host and exclusivity assumptions, and keep unknown credentials/creates even after publication becomes final. Prepared-but-not-dispatched work needs an explicit cancelled dispatch fence. This does not preserve the old ordinary engine or formats, and unsettled old resources do not prevent a genuinely separate new-model workspace.\n\nI also read the complete independent R0 verdict92abd211: approval for design adoption of all6 exacta3d artifacts with erratum2abe, not implementation approval, landing, task-ledger closure or adoption of R1-R4. Builder is the requester who owes ratification; do not satisfy its request as another party. Joint017018 promise2c25df2f is effective, with verdict still owed. Current R2 revision3 handoff8b16b3c1 has been read in full; its1248-line revised note/matrix/evidence are next for complete reading, so this assessment does not claim those bytes reconciled. R4 at2f83bf8c remains unfiled and not yet fully read. Sprint coordination5c020815 and the other planner's composed-MCP artifact work are left intact; no source, tests, installs, private diagnostic, provider action or deployment was performed or commissioned by this reading.","body":{"head":"de9a1d64b1788cb61ef048e2f6b753a7c8649fd9","no_git_artifact":"true","scope":"Whole R3 revision2 internal design reading and corrections, independent R0 verdict reading; no adoption or commissioning.","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0bcdeefc787284769771d0138675be77b79d9b77, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5c020815af528f564ce0e882b9b34bd870f0b61f, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8b16b3c137d4f9ca5997d03ab6a425eb500b4ed5, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1: